    {
     "type": "key",
     "style": "methode",
     "title": "Instruments and good practice",
     "md": "- **Metre rule**: reads to 1 mm (0.1 cm). **Vernier callipers** read to 0.1 mm and a **micrometer screw gauge** to 0.01 mm: used for small lengths and diameters.\n- Look **straight down** at the scale to avoid **parallax**; if the end of the rule is worn, start from a whole-number mark and subtract.\n- Check for **zero error** before measuring.\n- A **stopwatch** reads time; timing many swings reduces the error."
    },
    {
     "type": "key",
     "style": "retenir",
     "title": "Standard form and volumes",
     "md": "Very large or small numbers are written as **a × 10ⁿ** with 1 ≤ a < 10: 4500 m = 4.5 × 10³ m; 0.0032 kg = 3.2 × 10⁻³ kg. **Area of a rectangle** = length × width; **volume of a block** = length × width × height. Convert every length to the same unit before multiplying."
    },
    {
     "type": "key",
     "style": "pieges",
     "title": "Common mistakes",
     "md": "- Reading a rule from its end when the edge is damaged or when the object is not placed at zero.\n- Mixing units: 40 cm³ is **not** 40 m³ (1 m³ = 1 000 000 cm³).\n- **Accuracy** (close to the true value) and **precision** (readings close to each other) are not the same."
    },
    {
     "type": "example",
     "title": "Length from a rule",
     "statement": "A block lies along a rule from the 1.0 cm mark to the 6.3 cm mark. Find its length.",
     "steps": [
      "Length = right reading − left reading.",
      "6.3 − 1.0 = 5.3 cm."
     ],
     "answer": "5.3 cm"
    },
    {
     "type": "example",
     "title": "Volume in m³",
     "statement": "A block is 5.0 cm × 4.0 cm × 2.0 cm. Find its volume in cm³ and in m³.",
     "steps": [
      "Volume = 5.0 × 4.0 × 2.0 = 40 cm³.",
      "1 cm³ = 10⁻⁶ m³, so 40 cm³ = 4.0 × 10⁻⁵ m³."
     ],
     "answer": "40 cm³ = 4.0 × 10⁻⁵ m³"
    },
    {
     "type": "exercise",
     "ref": "form3-physics-measurement-e01"
    }
   ],
